<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold II of Belgium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a>–<a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Leopold II, the Builder King</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold II was the second king of the Belgians from 1865 to 1909 and sovereign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> from 1885 to 1908. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, the son of Leopold I, he commissioned so many buildings and public works that Belgians call him the Builder King. With the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> he claimed the Congo, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884–1885 granted him as his own colony. He ruled it through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/force-publique">Force Publique</a>, first for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and then for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> harvested by forced labour, with torture, murder and the cutting off of hands. Estimates of the Congolese population decline under his rule range from 1 million to 15 million.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-of-belgium.jpg" alt="Leopold II of Belgium" width="330" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bassano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leopold_II,_King_of_the_Belgians_by_Alexander_Bassano_(1889).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold was the second child of Leopold I and his second wife, Louise, a daughter of King Louis Philippe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His elder brother had died in infancy in 1834, so he was heir from birth and became Duke of Brabant at the age of five. He served in the army until his accession, reaching the rank of lieutenant-general, and sat in the Senate from 1855, where he urged the acquisition of colonies. On 22 August 1853 he married Marie Henriette of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>; their son died in 1869 aged nine, and the couple later separated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Leopold took the oath on 17 December 1865. The Liberals governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> until 1880 and passed the Frère-Orban Law of 1879 on free, secular primary schools; the Catholic Party won a majority in 1880 and restored state support to Catholic schools four years later. Social unrest and the rise of the Labour Party, formed in 1885, forced the adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a> in 1893. Laws also restricted child labour and gave workers compensation for accidents and Sundays off.</p><p>Much of his building was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, Ostend, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tervuren">Tervuren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, among it the Royal Museum for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> and Antwerpen-Centraal station. The historians Wm. Roger Louis and Adam Hochschild hold that Congo profits largely paid for it, though this is disputed. On 15 November 1902 the Italian anarchist Gennaro Rubino fired three shots at his procession and missed him.</p><p>Earlier colonial schemes failed: in 1866 he sought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-ii-of-spain">Isabella II of Spain</a>, and he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Roger Casement's report and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-d-morel">E. D. Morel</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-reform-association">Congo Reform Association</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-conan-doyle">Arthur Conan Doyle</a>, made the abuses an international issue. Under pressure from Parliament Leopold ceded the Free State to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in 1908 at a cost of 215.5 million francs, 50 million of it paid to him, and its archive was burned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leopold died at Laeken of an embolism on 17 December 1909, and the crown passed to Albert I, son of his brother Philippe. His funeral cortege was booed. His 44-year reign remains the longest in Belgian history. Statues were raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> mostly between the wars, in a period of colonial propaganda, and Hochschild's King Leopold's Ghost of 1999 reopened the debate on his legacy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold II of Belgium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
